    "id": "case-file",
    "title": "3. Canadian Technology Investigation (20%)",
    "name": "Canadian Technology Investigation",
    "summary": "Choose one documented technology system or automated decision process used in Canada. Follow your curiosity through the case. Your goal is not to prove a conclusion you already hold. Your goal is to discover how the system works, try the course ideas as ways of seeing it, and explain how the evidence changed or complicated your understanding.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><h3>Your exploration</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose one documented technology system or automated decision process used in Canada. Follow your curiosity through the case. Your goal is not to prove a conclusion you already hold. Your goal is to discover how the system works, try the course ideas as ways of seeing it, and explain how the evidence changed or complicated your understanding.</p><h3>Choose a case that makes you curious</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose a case from the course case bank, such as facial recognition in Canadian policing, risk assessment in federal corrections, or technology used in immigration or border systems. Ask me before using a different case. Begin with something you genuinely want to understand, question, or figure out.</p><h3>Build your inquiry trail</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Begin with one question you genuinely want to explore. Name the Canadian system, the institution using it, the decision it influences, and the people affected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Show a simple pathway with five parts: information in, a rule or decision, the institution responsible, the result, and a route to question or appeal it. A numbered text pathway is as acceptable as a diagram. Mark one uncertainty.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Use two credible sources, including at least one official, court, oversight, or other primary source. Assigned case-bank sources count. For each, give its title, working link and exact page, section or timestamp; explain one useful detail and one limit. An optional short quotation must be no more than 25 words.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Use two taught ideas, such as engineered inequity, default discrimination or coded exposure, to explain the case. Explain the ideas in your own words. If overlapping social positions affect the outcome, explain that connection without assuming every person has the same experience.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Compare the case with one of your Field Notes. Identify one similarity, one difference and one question. Return to the feedback on that entry while you work.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Explain what changed or became less certain as you explored. Separate what the evidence supports from what you cannot yet claim.</li></ul><h3>Keep evidence of your learning</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Include your initial question and date, two dated planning notes or draft snapshots, and one decision you changed with a reason. A short process note is enough; a separate research diary is not required.</p><h3>Asynchronous learning walkthrough</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose one way to show how you used evidence: a two-to-three-minute screen recording with captions or a transcript, OR two annotated screenshots or precise written descriptions with 150 to 200 words in total. Point to one source detail and one part of your pathway. Explain how a course idea changed what you noticed. Both options are asynchronous and use the same criteria; no live meeting or special permission is needed for the written option.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use the headings above for one exploration of about 600 to 800 words, plus your short evidence walkthrough and source list. The pathway and process notes may be brief bullet points. Word counts guide the scope; clear reasoning matters more than reaching an exact count.</p><h3>Use of generative AI</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Do not use generative AI to choose or research the case, find or summarize sources, plan the exploration, write or rewrite text, create the pathway, produce visuals, prepare a script, create narration, or produce the learning walkthrough. You may use ordinary spelling, grammar, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Privacy and accessibility</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Do not include private account information or identifying details about other people. Use readable headings, descriptive link text, alt text for meaningful images, and captions or a transcript for audio or video.</p><h3>Build on earlier feedback</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Before you start, revisit the feedback on Assessment 1. Choose one suggestion that can help your reasoning or use of evidence here. You do not need a grade or feedback on an assignment that is still being marked.</p><h3>How your exploration will be graded: 20 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Curiosity, inquiry trail, and change in understanding: 4 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Verified evidence, accurate source locations, and attention to uncertainty: 5 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">System pathway and explanation of how people, data, design, and institutions shape the outcome: 3 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Thoughtful use of course lenses, intersectionality, power, and the Field Note comparison: 5 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Process record, clear communication, accessibility, and asynchronous learning walkthrough: 3 points</li></ul><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select Start attempt. Write directly in the Submission editor using the task headings. A document upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Include your pathway, two-source trail, course explanation, Field Note comparison, conclusion and brief process record together.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Add your written or annotated walkthrough in the same editor. If you choose a recording, attach it with captions or a transcript, or provide a working Seneca OneDrive/Stream link.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">You may submit twice. The latest submitted attempt counts. Include the complete work in that attempt, select Submit, wait for confirmation, and keep the receipt. Saving a draft does not submit it.</li></ul><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: October 1, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: October 20, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends October 22, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
