    "id": "field-notes-2",
    "title": "4. Digital Systems Field Notes Portfolio 2 (10%)",
    "name": "Digital Systems Field Notes Portfolio 2",
    "summary": "Continue the private field-notes practice with three new moments from your own digital life. This phase asks you to notice how a system sorts, predicts, rewards, restricts, or defines a person or choice.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><h3>Purpose</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Continue the private field-notes practice with three new moments from your own digital life. This phase asks you to notice how a system sorts, predicts, rewards, restricts, or defines a person or choice.</p><h3>What to complete</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Post three new short, dated entries using the Weeks 8 and 9 material. Choose different low-risk digital moments between November 2 and November 15. You can revise your entries before the deadline.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Aim for 150 to 200 words per entry, or a one-to-two-minute audio entry with a transcript. Use the prompts as short headings; several brief sentences can address more than one prompt.</p><h3>Include these six parts in every entry</h3><ol style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Original example: Add one example you made or captured after removing personal details, or describe it precisely.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Your action: State exactly what you did.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">The system response: State what the system showed, allowed, blocked, ranked, recommended, predicted, or requested.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Course connection: Use one concept or assigned source from the relevant week. Explain the connection in your own words.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Consequence and power: Identify who gained an advantage, who carried a burden or risk, and what design choice produced the result.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Reflection: Explain how your interpretation has changed since Portfolio 1.</li></ol><h3>Privacy and authorship</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Remove names, account numbers, addresses, faces, and other identifying details.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">You may describe a trace instead of uploading it.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Do not test a system with a made-up identity or ask another person to reveal private information.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Specific personal examples and accurate course connections matter more than polished language.</li></ul><h3>Build on earlier feedback</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Before you start, revisit the feedback on Assessment 3. Choose one suggestion that can help your reasoning or use of evidence here. You do not need a grade or feedback on an assignment that is still being marked.</p><h3>How this journal is graded: 10 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Specific examples and evidence: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Accurate course concepts and sources: 2.5 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">How the system creates consequences and distributes power: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Reflection and growth: 1.5 points.</li></ul><h3>How to post your journal entries</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this journal in Blackboard and find Entries. Start a separate entry for each required date or example.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Write directly in the journal editor. Put the entry number and date at the top. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose an audio entry, attach the recording and a transcript, or add a Seneca-accessible recording link.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Post, then reopen the entry to confirm it is there. You may edit your entries through the submission window stated below. Only you and the teaching team can read this journal.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: November 2, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: November 13, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends November 15, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
