    "id": "repair",
    "title": "5. Design the Repair (20%)",
    "name": "Design the Repair",
    "summary": "Choose one real digital system problem you documented in a Field Note or the Canadian Technology Investigation. Redesign one part of that system so the repair changes the decision process, not only the wording around it.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose one real digital system problem you documented in a Field Note or the Canadian Technology Investigation. Redesign one part of that system so the repair changes the decision process, not only the wording around it.</p><h3>Submit three connected pieces</h3><ol style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Before-and-after model: Show the current pathway beside your repaired pathway. Identify the exact point where your design intervenes.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Design rationale: Submit either a two to three minute narrated walkthrough or a 400 to 600 word written rationale. Explain what you changed, why it addresses the part of the system you identified, and what trade-off remains.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Decision log: List three design choices you made, one alternative you rejected, and the reason for each decision.</li></ol><h3>Test the repair</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Consider your proposal from these three points of view:</p><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">a person most likely to carry the original burden or risk;</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">a frontline worker or institution that must use or administer the system; and</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">a community, rights, or accountability perspective.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">For each position, state what improves, what could still fail, and what you would change after the test.</p><h3>Measure success and failure</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Name one observable measure that would show the repair is working.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Name one warning sign that would show the repair is reproducing or moving the harm.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Name one possible unintended consequence.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Explain what evidence would be needed before a real organization should adopt the repair.</li></ul><h3>Keep the design manageable</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">A before-and-after numbered text pathway is as acceptable as a diagram. You are proposing a small change, not building software or writing professional policy. Reuse the evidence from your earlier investigation; no new field research, recruitment or testing with real people is required. The three viewpoints are a desk-based thought exercise, not permission to speak for those groups.</p><h3>Build on earlier feedback</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Before you start, revisit the feedback on Assessment 3. Choose one suggestion that can help your reasoning or use of evidence here. You do not need a grade or feedback on an assignment that is still being marked.</p><h3>How it will be graded: 20 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Problem and mechanism: 5 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Repair specificity and feasibility: 6 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Stakeholder stress test and equity: 4 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Measures and unintended effects: 3 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Communication and decision trace: 2 points</li></ul><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select Start attempt. Write or paste your written work into the Submission editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose a visual format, insert it with a text description or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Submit, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Privacy and accessibility</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You control what personal information you share. Remove identifying details about other people. Use readable headings and descriptive links. Add a text description for meaningful visuals, and captions or a transcript for audio or video. Your grade reflects your learning and reasoning; private disclosure and production effects do not earn extra marks.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: November 16, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: November 27, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends November 29, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
